contact:
You cannot write to, or trade with, a city you have never reached. A settlement becomes reachable — **permanently** — once one of these has happened:

- you own it;
- **one of your messengers has physically arrived there**, ever — even if it has long since come home;
- one of your marches has passed over it.

Contact is earned by walking, and once earned it never lapses. Sending a messenger to a stranger's city is not only a letter — it is the act that opens [[trade]] with them for good.

gossip:
**Gossip (🗣)** is what travellers, merchants and soldiers say about the world beyond your sight — who rules where, what happened, which cities exist.

Rumour is useful but blurred:

- it tells you **that** a city exists and **roughly** where, deliberately imprecise;
- it can be old news;
- it does **not** give you [[contact]] — you still have to reach a city before you can write to it or trade with it.

food-when-there-is-not-enough:
If the available food and emergency livestock run out and the game day's need is still unmet:

- the settlement **loses part of its population that game day**, and keeps losing while the shortfall lasts;
- it shrinks until what its land yields can feed those who are left — the ground right around a city always feeds about a hundred;
- below **50 people** — starved there, or emptied by drafting soldiers — **the settlement collapses and is gone.**

You get an urgent **CivilianStarvationStarted** notification when hunger is first observed. It records the game day, city and how much of that game day's meal was missing, in food. The latest start stays above ordinary history in Notifications until that city's food supply has recovered steadily. You get **CivilianStarvationEnded** after a run of fully fed game days; the old start then returns to ordinary history. Hunger returning during that recovery window belongs to the same warning. A new spell after recovery starts a new pair of notices, rather than repeating the warning every game day.

sieges-when-it-happens-to-you:
You get a notification when a siege begins and when it is lifted. The City drawer carries a red banner naming who holds the approaches, and the map draws a dark cloud over the city.

What helps:

- full [[food|city food stores]] — the city eats its grain, fish, oil and wine while the fields are cut off;
- food brought in from outside by [[transfers|transfer]] — if it can get through;
- a relief force that drives the besiegers off the hexes ([[battle]]).

If the city falls, see [[occupation]].
